1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,398
Run the sampler once.

2
00:00:01,398 --> 00:00:03,861
Then run it again with the same seed.

3
00:00:03,861 --> 00:00:05,525
The counts match exactly.

4
00:00:05,525 --> 00:00:08,254
Does that agreement make the result true?

5
00:00:08,254 --> 00:00:08,454
No.

6
00:00:08,454 --> 00:00:19,703
In the next few minutes, I will show what a seed controls, reproduce Chapter One's actual output, and mark the boundary between replaying a process and checking a claim.

7
00:00:19,703 --> 00:00:23,830
This narration is synthetic and generated locally with Kokoro.

8
00:00:23,830 --> 00:00:29,354
The course sample function receives logits, a count, a seed, and a temperature.

9
00:00:29,354 --> 00:00:32,711
It first converts the logits into probabilities.

10
00:00:32,711 --> 00:00:38,165
Then random dot Random, with the seed, creates a local pseudorandom generator.

11
00:00:38,165 --> 00:00:43,969
That generator makes weighted selections, and a Counter totals the selected labels.

12
00:00:43,969 --> 00:00:49,004
The seed starts the generator; it never changes the probability formula.

13
00:00:49,004 --> 00:00:52,507
I ran the unmodified Chapter One reference program.

14
00:00:52,507 --> 00:00:59,718
Its constructed input is logits one, two, and three; count one thousand; temperature one; and seed seven.

15
00:00:59,718 --> 00:01:08,440
It prints label zero, one hundred two times; label one, two hundred sixty-eight times; and label two, six hundred thirty times.

16
00:01:08,440 --> 00:01:13,866
These are observed counts from the real program, not invented animation values.

17
00:01:13,866 --> 00:01:19,832
Now call the same function a second time with seed seven and every other input unchanged.

18
00:01:19,832 --> 00:01:25,998
The second run again gives one hundred two, two hundred sixty-eight, and six hundred thirty.

19
00:01:25,998 --> 00:01:28,143
The equality check returns true.

20
00:01:28,143 --> 00:01:37,192
This is the useful promise: when debugging, I can reset the generator to the same starting state and replay this particular experiment.

21
00:01:37,192 --> 00:01:40,542
Next, I changed only the seed from seven to eight.

22
00:01:40,542 --> 00:01:44,763
The logits, probabilities, count, and temperature stayed fixed.

23
00:01:44,763 --> 00:01:53,540
The observed counts changed to label zero, seventy-six; label one, two hundred thirty-nine; and label two, six hundred eighty-five.

24
00:01:53,540 --> 00:01:56,890
The distribution used for sampling did not change.

25
00:01:56,890 --> 00:02:01,982
The particular pseudorandom sequence, and therefore this finite sample, did.

26
00:02:01,982 --> 00:02:10,050
Chapter One's test calls sample with logits one and two, then compares that result with another call using the same defaults.

27
00:02:10,050 --> 00:02:14,181
The test passes because the default seed is seven in both calls.

28
00:02:14,181 --> 00:02:16,053
That is a repeatability test.

29
00:02:16,053 --> 00:02:21,281
It checks whether this implementation can replay its output under the same setup.

30
00:02:21,281 --> 00:02:24,444
It does not compare any label with an answer key.

31
00:02:24,444 --> 00:02:28,969
Look at the function's inputs: logits, count, seed, and temperature.

32
00:02:28,969 --> 00:02:33,760
There is no source, answer key, evidence check, or correctness argument.

33
00:02:33,760 --> 00:02:39,683
So the function can tell us which labels were selected and whether a run can be repeated.

34
00:02:39,683 --> 00:02:43,742
It cannot tell us whether a selected label describes reality.

35
00:02:43,742 --> 00:02:47,602
Repeating a mistake perfectly still preserves the mistake.

36
00:02:47,602 --> 00:02:50,353
The complete claim is small but important.

37
00:02:50,353 --> 00:02:58,016
A seed fixes the starting state of the pseudorandom selection process, so a run can be replayed under the same setup.

38
00:02:58,016 --> 00:03:03,715
Changing the seed can change the observed sample while leaving the probabilities fixed.

39
00:03:03,715 --> 00:03:07,514
This explanation establishes repeatability of the process.

40
00:03:07,514 --> 00:03:13,016
It does not establish truth, correctness, or factual support for any sampled output.
